"The disjointed letters (huruf al-muqatta'at) such as Ta-Sin at the beginning of the surah are divine secrets known to Allah. These are the verses of the clear Quran. Sent down as guidance and good tidings for the believers, this Book describes the overturn that will befall the oppressors.\n\nThe believers establish prayer, give zakat, and firmly believe in the Hereafter. For them there is an excellent outcome. As for those who do not believe in the Hereafter, their deeds have been made fair-seeming to them, and their hearts have become blind. They will suffer a terrible punishment both in this world and in the Hereafter.\n\nThe Quran is being sent down by Allah, the All-Wise, the All-Knowing. An example of this is the story of Moses (Musa): while traveling with his family, Moses saw a fire and told them that he would bring them news or a burning brand from it. When he approached the fire, he was called to and informed that the area had been blessed.\n\nMoses was commanded to cast down his staff, and the staff moved like a snake. Moses fled in fear, but Allah told him not to fear. The prophets do not fear in the presence of Allah; except for those who do wrong, those who repent are forgiven. Moses was given nine signs (miracles), and Pharaoh and his people were destroyed.\n\nThen the story of David and Solomon is recounted: Allah granted them knowledge, and they gave thanks for this blessing. Solomon inherited from David and declared that they had been taught the language of birds and had been given a share of everything. This is a manifest favor.\n\nSolomon's army consisted of jinn, humans, and birds. When they arrived at a valley of ants, an ant warned the others to enter their dwellings lest they be crushed by Solomon and his army. Hearing these words, Solomon smiled and prayed to Allah to enable him to be grateful, to do righteous deeds, and to be admitted into His mercy.\n\nWhile inspecting the birds, Solomon could not find the hoopoe (hudhud) and threatened to punish it severely or slaughter it. The hoopoe arrived shortly after and said that it had brought important news from Sheba (Saba'): the Queen of Sheba and her people were prostrating to the sun instead of Allah. Satan had led them astray.\n\nThe hoopoe related that the queen had a magnificent throne and that her people did not prostrate to Allah. Yet it is Allah who brings to light what is hidden in the heavens and the earth. He is the Lord of the Mighty Throne. Solomon ordered the hoopoe to take his letter to Sheba in order to investigate whether it was speaking the truth.\n\nThe letter began with the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, and called upon the queen and her people to submit to Solomon. The queen gathered the notables, consulted them about the matter, and sought their opinion.\n\nThe hoopoe comes to Solomon (peace be upon him), saying that it has learned something he did not know, and reports that it has brought important news from Sheba. It relates that a woman rules as queen in Sheba, that she has been granted everything, and that she possesses a magnificent throne. Solomon (peace be upon him) attaches importance not to this news, but to hearing that she and her people prostrate to the sun, and he writes and sends a letter to investigate whether the hoopoe is speaking the truth. For if the person who brings news is not trustworthy, its veracity must be investigated (al-Hujurat 49:6).\n\nUpon receiving the letter, the queen gathers the notables and asks them to give her counsel on this matter. They reply that they are powerful and fierce in battle, but that the command belongs to her. Reminding them that when kings enter a land they ruin it and humiliate its noble people, the queen decides to send a gift to Solomon to avoid war.\n\nSolomon (peace be upon him) rejects the gift sent to him, says that what Allah has given him is better, and threatens the envoys. Then, asking who could bring her throne before the queen and her people arrive in submission, an ifrit from among the jinn says he can bring it before Solomon rises from his seat. But a person who possessed knowledge from the Book brings the throne in the blink of an eye. It is narrated that this person was Asaf ibn Barkhiya or Solomon (peace be upon him) himself. The bringing of the throne is regarded as a favor from Allah, and Solomon (peace be upon him) gives thanks.\n\nSolomon alters the appearance of the throne to test whether the queen will recognize it. When the queen sees the throne, she says that it is just like hers, noting that they had been given knowledge beforehand and had submitted. What she used to worship besides Allah had held her back. When the queen enters a palace paved with crystal, she mistakes the floor for a pool of water and pulls up her skirts; Solomon tells her that it is a transparent paved floor. Thereupon, the queen realizes that she has wronged herself and submits to Allah, the Lord of the worlds.\n\nNext, the story of Salih (peace be upon him), who was sent to the people of Thamud, is recounted. His people accuse him and those with him of being an evil omen. Salih replies that their omen is with Allah. A gang of nine persons in the city plots to kill Salih and his family, but Allah foils their plan and destroys them all, saving those who believed.\n\nIn the story of Lot (peace be upon him), his people commit shameful indecency. Lot warns them, but they seek to drive Lot's family out of their land. Allah saves Lot and his family, while his wife remains behind among those who are punished. A dreadful rain is poured down upon the people.\n\nAfter these stories, Allah commands the Prophet to praise Him and send peace upon His chosen servants. The polytheists are asked: Is Allah better, or that which they associate with Him? Then the proofs demonstrating Allah's power are enumerated: He is the One who created the heavens and the earth, sent down water and grew gardens, made the earth fit for habitation, caused rivers to flow, created mountains, responds to the distressed, guides in the darkness, sends the winds as glad tidings, originates creation and repeats it, and provides sustenance from the heaven and the earth. After each of these, it is asked, \"Is there any deity alongside Allah?\" thereby highlighting the ignorance of the polytheists.\n\nSay: None in the heavens and the earth knows the unseen except Allah. Not only do they lack knowledge concerning the Hereafter, but they are also in doubt and blindness regarding it.\n\nThe particle 'am' (or) in verse 60 is explained: connected (muttasil) 'am' denotes two alternatives, while severed (munqati') 'am' carries the meaning of transition (like 'bal') and interrogation. Although 'or' is used in translation, it does not fully convey the sense; expressions like 'nay, rather' are sometimes preferred. Exegetes have interpreted the expression in the verse 'Or the One who created the heavens and the earth?' as meaning 'Is the Creator better, or that which they associate as partners?'\n\nIn verse 60, the final cause (illat al-gha'iyyah / teleological cause) is discussed: the sending down of water is for the benefit of humanity. Although philosophers deny a final cause in divine acts, this is accepted as an argument from wisdom. There is benefit and purpose in Allah's acts; creation is not in vain. The connection between water and life demonstrates Allah's wisdom.\n\nIn the continuation of verse 60, it is emphasized that beautiful gardens are grown with the water sent down, and that humans could not cause even a single tree to grow on their own. This is manifest proof that there is no deity besides Allah. With the question 'Is there any deity alongside Allah?', the deviation of the polytheists is exposed.\n\nIn verse 61, the power of Allah is described: He made the earth fit for dwelling, caused rivers to flow, set firm mountains, and placed a barrier between the two seas. These objective signs demonstrate His oneness. Most of the polytheists do not know these realities.\n\nIn verse 62, it is Allah Who answers the prayer of the distressed one and makes you successors on earth. This is glad tidings both individually and socially; it points to the future dominance of Islam. The question 'Is there a god besides Allah?' is repeated.\n\nIn verses 63-64, it is Allah Who guides in the darkness of land and sea, Who sends the winds as heralds before His mercy, Who originates creation and will then repeat it, and Who provides sustenance from heaven and earth. In this fifth question, it is emphasized that the order of creation necessitates a single deity. The polytheists are asked to bring their proof.\n\nIn verses 65-66, it is stated that only Allah knows the unseen (ghayb), and that although people have received successive knowledge concerning the Hereafter, they remain in doubt. The word 'iddaraka' signifies the continuous, uninterrupted arrival of information; yet, because they are blind, they do not wish to see.\n\nIn verses 67-75, the deniers' rejection of the resurrection and their dismissal of it as 'fables of the ancients' are recounted. Patience is advised to the Prophet; tidings are given that a portion of the punishment will come soon. Allah's grace and knowledge are emphasized, and it is stated that the Quran will settle the disputes of the Children of Israel.\n\nVerses 76-79 declare that the Quran is guidance and mercy for the believers, that their Lord will deliver His judgment, and that since the Prophet stands upon the manifest truth, he should place his trust in Allah.\n\nIn verses 80-81, it is explained that calling out to the dead (the disbelievers) is of no avail, and that only those who submit wholeheartedly can hear. In verse 82, 'Dabbat al-Ard' (the Beast of the Earth), one of the signs of the Day of Judgment, is expounded upon. There are different interpretations as to whether this creature is a speaking beast or a human being; in the famous hadith, it is narrated that it will distinguish the believers with the staff of Moses (Musa) and the seal of Solomon in its possession.\n\nIn verses 83-86, the Day is depicted when those who denied the verses from every community will be gathered. Allah will question them, and because of the wrongdoing they committed, the word will be fulfilled against them, leaving them unable to speak. Attention is drawn to the lessons in the creation of night and day.\n\nIn verse 86, Allah Almighty draws attention to the creation of night and day, saying, 'Have they not seen?' What is meant here is seeing with the eye of the heart; for the fact that night and day were created with the wisdom of tranquility and activity can only be understood through reasoning. This order consists of clear signs pointing to Allah's existence, His oneness, prophethood, and the Hereafter. In terms of human life in particular, the creation of night for rest and day for labor demonstrates the existence of a Creator Who knows all things and does whatever He wills.\n\nIn verse 87, it is reported that on the Day the Trumpet (Sur) is blown, whoever is in the heavens and whoever is on the earth will be stricken with terror, except those whom Allah wills. There are different views regarding the Sur: some interpret it as the plural of suwar (forms), meaning the breathing of souls, while others interpret it as a figurative horn. According to the majority, however, the Sur is a horn that will be blown by Israfil, and it will be blown three times: the first for terror, the second for death, and the third for resurrection. At the first blowing mentioned in this verse, everyone is shaken with dread, yet those whom Allah wills are safe. It is not certain who these are; it is most appropriate to explain them with 'those who are secure from fear' in verse 89.\n\nIn verses 88-89, it is described that while the mountains are thought to be stationary, they pass away like clouds. Commentators generally interpret this expression as referring to the Day of Judgment, yet in that case the expression 'you think' would not be fitting. Some scholars argue that this movement continues constantly in this world, and that the mountains are changing and being renewed at every moment. According to Elmalılı, this verse directs thought to the Day of Judgment and the resurrection by demonstrating the continuous coming into being and passing away of entities. In the particles of mountains there is a transformation like clouds, and through Allah's flawless artistry, this order operates with wisdom. Those who come with goodness on that Day will be secure from fear, whereas those who come with evil will be cast into the Fire.\n\nIn verse 90, it is stated that those who bring evil deeds will be cast into Hell, and it is said, 'Will you be recompensed for anything other than what you used to do?' This demonstrates that deeds will be requited with justice.\n\nIn verses 91-93, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) is commanded: worship only the Lord of Mecca, be of the Muslims, and recite the Quran. Whoever accepts guidance does so for his own good, and whoever strays, he is only a warner. Then it is stated, 'Praise be to Allah; He will show you His signs, and you will recognize them.' This is the glad tidings of the triumphs promised in the Quran and the future victories of Islam. Indeed, from Badr to Mehmed the Conqueror (Fatih), Selim I (Yavuz), and Suleiman the Magnificent (Kanuni), these promises were fulfilled. The victories of Gallipoli (Çanakkale), Sakarya, and İnönü, as well as the liberation of Izmir, are also among the signs that Allah has shown in this century.\n\nAt the end of the surah, it is emphasized that although Islam experiences ups and downs like night and day, its future is bright. The hadith 'Islam began as something strange and will return to being strange, so glad tidings to the strangers' expresses glad tidings rather than despair. That is to say, Islam will not be extinguished but will be reborn. What falls upon the believers is to praise Allah, await His signs, and believe that their Lord is not unaware of what they do."